<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kihapiʻilani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kihapiilani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kihapiʻilani was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in the 16th century, a son of Piʻilani. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</a> of the island of Hawaiʻi he overthrew his elder brother Lono-a-Piʻilani and took power. Tradition credits him with the paved trail, later called the King's Highway, that circled much of Maui, and with work on the great temple platform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piilanihale">Piʻilanihale</a>.</p></div>
